# Coach speech (Hungarian): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Hungarian needs.
# Register: the coach says "te" to the player (tegezés: "Lépj!", "a huszárod"); the game menus are
# formal ("Ön"). The coach speaks of himself in the 1st person ("én", "a huszárom"). Hungarian has no
# grammatical gender: no agreement with the coach, the player or a piece.
# Two rules for every line of every file (see also the glossary):
# - Never attach a suffix or the article a/az to {sq}, {move}, {best}, {reply}, {line}, {eval} or a
#   number: "a"/"az" and the vowel harmony of a suffix depend on how the voice says them ("a d4" but
#   "az e4", "e4-re" but "e6-ra", "5-öt" but "6-ot"). Put the case on a noun next to them, with no
#   article before a square (as chess books write "e4-re lép"): "{sq} mezőre", "{sq} mezőn", "{best}
#   lépéssel"; or let them stand alone: "Jobb volt: {best}.", "{reply} után..."
# - A number before a noun uses a count key ({pts:point_acc} = "3 pontot") or the form {n:attr}
#   ("két lépés", never "kettő lépés"); bare {n} is the standalone word ("kettő").

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns: gyalog, huszár, futó, bástya, vezér, király (no gender). A piece placeholder is a whole noun
# phrase: article + noun + possessive suffix (2nd person "-d/-od/-ed", 1st person "-m/-om/-em") + case
# suffix, so every possessed form is a phrase.<owner>.<type>.<form> key. All pieces start with a
# consonant: the article is always "a" (and "A" at the start of a sentence, capitalised by the program).
# The Analysis mode names a piece by its side: "a világos huszár", "a sötét vezért" (the colour word
# never changes; the case suffix goes on the noun: piece.<type>.<case> inside owner.white/black.<case>).
# Forms (each defined for the six pieces and the four owners your, my, white, black):
#   nom   a huszárod / a vezérem / a világos huszár   "{your} nem így lép." Also before every
#         postposition: "{my} mellett", "{your} mögött", "{my} felé", "{t1} ellen", "{my} után".
#   acc   a huszárodat / a vezéremet (-t)              "Üsd le {my:acc}!"
#   dat   a huszárodnak / a vezéremnek (-nak/-nek)     "{my:dat} nincs szabad mezője." (Hungarian
#         "to have" is dative + van: "{your:dat} nincs lépése" = your king has no move.)
#   ins   a huszároddal / a vezéremmel (-val/-vel)     "Próbáld {your:ins}!", "Lépj {your:ins}!"
#   sub   a huszárodra / a vezéremre (-ra/-re, onto)   "Vigyázz {your:sub}!"
#   sup   a huszárodon / a vezéremen (-on/-en/-ön, on) "Sok múlik {your:sup}."
#   del   a huszárodról / a vezéremről (-ról/-ről)     "Ne feledkezz meg {your:del}!"
#   ill   a huszárodba / a vezérembe (-ba/-be, into)   "Ne szeress bele {your:ill}!"
#   ine   a huszárodban / a vezéremben (-ban/-ben, in) "Bízz {your:ine}!"
#   ela   a huszárodból / a vezéremből (-ból/-ből)     "{my:ela} vezér lesz." (my pawn becomes a queen)
#   all   a huszárodhoz / a vezéremhez (-hoz/-hez/-höz) "{your} közel van {my:all}."
#   ade   a huszárodnál / a vezéremnél (-nál/-nél: at, than)  "{my} kevesebbet ér {your:ade}."
#   abl   a huszárodtól / a vezéremtől (-tól/-től, from)      "{my} messze van {your:abl}."
#   ter   a huszárodig / a vezéremig (-ig, as far as)  "Menj egészen {my:ter}!"
#   cau   a huszárodért / a vezéremért (-ért, for)     "Odaadom {my:acc} {your:cau}."
#   noart, noart_<case>   the same without the article: huszárod, huszárodat ... / világos huszár,
#         világos huszárt ... For a word between the article and the noun: "a kötött {your:noart}",
#         "a saját {your:noart_acc}", "az én {my:noart}" (the article before such a word is yours).
#   nopos, nopos_<case>   the article and the noun, no owner: a huszár, a huszárt, a huszárral ...
#         When the owner is obvious: "Lépj {your:nopos_ins}!" ("Lépj a huszárral!").
#   bare  the noun alone, nominative: huszár.          "Ez egy {your:bare}." ("Ez egy huszár.")
#   pron_<case>   the 3rd-person pronoun standing for the piece: pron_dat neki, pron_ins vele, pron_sub
#         rá, pron_sup rajta, pron_del róla, pron_ill bele, pron_ine benne, pron_ela belőle, pron_all
#         hozzá, pron_ade nála, pron_abl tőle, pron_cau érte. "Figyelj {your:pron_sub}!" ("Figyelj rá!").
#         They are the same words for every piece and owner, so a line may also write the word itself.
#         There is no nominative or accusative pronoun: Hungarian drops "it" as subject and object (the
#         verb says it: "Kötésben van.", "Üsd le!"); postpositions take their personal forms, written as
#         words: mögötte, előtte, mellette, ellene, felé, utána, körülötte.

